    <blockquote class="inline_help">
        <p>This setting controls what network interface is used for the data volume metrics shown in the dashboard and footer.</p>
        <p>It is also the network interface that is used for the primary network interface data volume notifications and user actions.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <dl>
        <dt>Show Virtual Network Interfaces:</dt>
        <dd>
            <select id="VIFACES" name="VIFACES" size="1">
                <?=mk_option($dvm_config_vifaces, "disable", "No");?>
                <?=mk_option($dvm_config_vifaces, "enable", "Yes");?>
            </select>
        </dd>
    </dl>

    <blockquote class="inline_help">
        <p>This setting controls if virtual network interfaces should also be displayed on this page.</p>
        <p>Not all virtual network interfaces are monitorable and some data reported for such interfaces may be inconsistent.</p>
        <p>Some virtual interfaces will show inverse traffic, e.g. RX as TX, because first the physical interface receives (RX) the traffic (e.g. from the internet) and then the virtual interface sends (TX) that traffic to its final destination (e.g. a Docker or VM).</p>
    </blockquote>

    <dl>
        <dt>Match Virtual Network Interfaces to Services:</dt>
        <dd>
            <select id="MATCHVIRTS" name="MATCHVIRTS" size="1">
                <?=mk_option($dvm_config_match_virts, "disable", "No");?>
                <?=mk_option($dvm_config_match_virts, "enable", "Yes");?>
            </select>
        </dd>
    </dl>

    <blockquote class="inline_help">
        <p>This setting will <u>attempt</u> to match any virtual interfaces with their respective Dockers and VMs, in order to better be able to read the metrics.</p>
        <p>Please note that virtual network interfaces do not persist between Docker container restarts and a different interface may be assigned after a container restarts.</p>
        <p>It is therefore best to set up thresholds using actual physical network interfaces (e.g. eth0) and not rely on virtual network interfaces for notifications and user actions.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <dl>
